&amp;#x200B;

The horn's not that bad though. It's solid with a team who depends heavily on stamina. The stamina buffs easily outrunning attack [PRODUCT]. [ORGANIZATION],bows, and [PRODUCT]. And it helps increase survivability with full stun negation, stam recover, and evasion +speed boost. The speed boost is helpful when you gotta deal with fast foes like [PERSON], [ORGANIZATION], and [ORGANIZATION]. If your terms for a Good song play list is attack up [LOCATION], then... \*shrug\* you do you.

&amp;#x200B;

Cry of Night has [PERCENT] affinity off the bat if you need a damage build. Which means that's a few Expert gems you do not need to slot any longer. 

&amp;#x200B;

Mad Bone Horn is solid in it's own right. And I already have it. But if I am only going to have [CARDINAL] of the songs up for most of the time. I'm not going to bother to pull it out unless I enter situations where it pays to have the other songs.

I see a few things here.

&amp;#x200B;

Are you making use of [ORGANIZATION]'s Bardic Inspiration? It's a +1 to all attacks, damage, and saving throws. It doesn't sound like much, but it takes you a long ways fast. This is applied to all friendlies within [CARDINAL]ft of you.

&amp;#x200B;

Also, [ORGANIZATION] heavily punishes ranged users who fire while in melee. Or fire into melee.  The penalty is -4. You can massively reduce their odds to hit while also gaining an opportunity of attack by engaging them in melee. It's the same concept with spell casters. Later into the game when you start getting a party, it's a good idea to get someone who can charge through or flank around to kill mages and archers. As they will eventually start getting feats where they pose a serious threat.
